---
// /operating-model-template: the Use Cases page for people searching for a template. One card per
// genome in the corpus, built from the corpus alone, so a new genome adds its own card. Each card
// opens that company's page in the Playground. The search title and description come from the
// keyword registry.
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import WaitlistForm from '~/components/widgets/WaitlistForm.astro';
import { genomeEntries } from '~/utils/genome-pages';
import { seoFor } from '~/utils/seo';
import { getCanonical } from '~/utils/permalinks';

const entries = genomeEntries();
const metadata = {
  ...seoFor('/operating-model-template'),
  openGraph: {
    images: [
      { url: String(getCanonical('/operating-model-template.png')).replace(/\/$/, ''), width: 1200, height: 628 },
    ],
  },
};

const COUNTS = [
  { key: 'roles', one: 'role', many: 'roles' },
  { key: 'processes', one: 'process', many: 'processes' },
  { key: 'rules', one: 'rule', many: 'rules' },
  { key: 'systems', one: 'system', many: 'systems' },
] as const;
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 pb-4 pt-16 sm:px-6">
    <div
      class="prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert prose-headings:font-heading prose-headings:tracking-tight mx-auto max-w-3xl"
    >
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-wide text-primary">Operating Model Template</p>
      <h1>An Operating Model Template, Already Filled In</h1>
      <p>
        Most operating model templates are a slide of empty boxes. Each one below is a whole company instead: the roles
        and who they report to, the process with a role on every step, the rules on those steps, and the systems that
        carry the data. Open one to see how the company works, then download it as the starting point for yours.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="not-prose mt-10 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {
        entries.map((e) => (
          <li class="flex">
            <a
              href={`/playground/${e.key}/`}
              class="group flex w-full flex-col rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 transition hover:border-primary dark:border-slate-700/60 dark:bg-slate-900"
              data-genome-card={e.key}
            >
              <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-primary">{e.industry}</span>
              <span class="mt-1 font-heading text-xl font-bold dark:text-slate-100">{e.orgName}</span>
              <span class="mt-1 text-xs font-medium text-muted dark:text-slate-400" data-provenance>
                {e.provenanceLabel}
              </span>
              <span class="mt-3 flex-1 text-sm text-muted dark:text-slate-300">{e.description}</span>
              <span class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-4 gap-2 text-center text-xs text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
                {COUNTS.map((c) => (
                  <span>
                    <strong class="block text-lg text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100" data-count={c.key}>
                      {e.counts[c.key]}
                    </strong>
                    {e.counts[c.key] === 1 ? c.one : c.many}
                  </span>
                ))}
              </span>
              <span class="mt-4 text-sm font-semibold text-primary group-hover:underline">Open the company →</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <div
      class="prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert prose-headings:font-heading prose-headings:tracking-tight mx-auto mt-14 max-w-3xl"
    >
      <h2>A Business Operating Model Template That Runs</h2>
      <p>
        A business operating model template only helps if it stays true after the workshop. Each company here is written
        as DNA, so every role reports to another role, every step names who does it and which operation it performs, and
        every rule sits on the operation it governs. The build checks each one against the published DNA schemas, so
        what you download is valid DNA, not a drawing of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each card says whether its company is fictional or modeled from public sources. Each company uses the vocabulary
        its industry already publishes, and its page names those standards.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each company page also gives you a prompt that starts an agent from that model, so you can draft your own in
        conversation. For the case behind a runnable model, read the&#32;
        <a href="/operating-model/">target operating model</a> page. To compare companies lens by lens, open the&#32;
        <a href="/playground/">Playground</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pb-16 sm:px-6">
    <WaitlistForm preselect="operations" />
  </div>
</Layout>
